<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the notarized macOS build.

  Notarization requires the hardened runtime (codesign's "options runtime"
  flag), and the hardened runtime denies JIT by default. cleave needs JIT:
  yara-x compiles its rules to WebAssembly and runs them through
  wasmtime/cranelift, which maps executable pages at load time. Without
  allow-jit, a hardened-runtime build dies the first time a ruleset loads,
  i.e. on anything that actually scans.

  allow-jit alone is not enough. A binary signed and notarized with only
  allow-jit (spctl reports "Notarized Developer ID") runs the version and help
  flags fine, yet any actual scan is killed in about a second:

      signal:      SIGKILL (Code Signature Invalid)
      termination: CODESIGNING / Invalid Page
      frame:       cleave::yara_engine::YaraEngine::scan_bytes_with_inline

  Re-signing the same binary with nothing changed but the entitlements isolates
  it: allow-jit alone reproduces the SIGKILL, allow-jit plus
  allow-unsigned-executable-memory scans cleanly. cranelift maps executable
  pages that MAP_JIT plus pthread_jit_write_protect_np does not cover, and
  allow-jit cannot authorize them on its own.

  This is also why the smoke test in build.yml runs a real scan: the version
  and help flags never compile a ruleset, so they never touch the JIT and pass
  even when signing is broken.

  Keep this comment free of doubled hyphens. They are illegal inside an XML
  comment, and codesign's AMFI parser rejects the whole file ("Failed to parse
  entitlements: AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error") even though plutil accepts it.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
